<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old Nubian</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Old Nubian is the medieval written form of the Nubian language, used in the Christian kingdoms of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> from about the 8th to the 15th century. Written in an adapted Coptic alphabet, it survives in church texts, letters, legal documents and graffiti, many found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasr-ibrim">Qasr Ibrim</a>. It was used alongside Greek and Coptic in Makuria and its successor Dotawo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-nubian.jpg" alt="Old Nubian" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parchment_page_of_a_book,_Liber_Institionis_Michaelis_Archangeli,_written_in_Old_Nubian._9th-10_century_CE._From_Qasr_Ibrahim,_Egypt._British_Museum._EA_71305.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
